 71	### Reading the timings
 72	
 73	Timings are from headless Chrome on one desktop machine. The scale probe attempts three runs per size and stops that size after the first failure. The November 22 comparison logs one-minute load before each scale attempt and streaming run. The report uses main-thread task time as the browser reports it (through the DevTools protocol), which counts work however a library schedules it but not rasterization on other threads. The older "time to second frame" figure is still recorded as `toPaintMs`; it cannot resolve below about 35 ms and stops counting before entry animations finish. The streaming producer sends one update per animation frame, so it is not an independent 60 Hz feed. WebGL ran on a software rasterizer. The performance plan replaces all of this with a stricter method ([docs/04-plan-performance.md](../docs/04-plan-performance.md)).
236	## 5. Performance budgets
237	
238	These numbers are provisional. They are design targets taken from Phase 1's order-of-magnitude measurements, and they become release gates only after the benchmark harness built in M0 has measured a working Axylo slice in M1 and the limits have been recalibrated and frozen (plan, M1 and M6). Each budget names its workload, metric and statistic so that two people measuring the same build get the same verdict.
239	
240	| ID | Workload | Metric | Statistic | Provisional limit | Phase 1 reference |
241	|---|---|---|---|---|---|
242	| P1 | `trend` (3×120), default options, reference desktop | Main-thread task time from the call until the chart is complete | Median of 20 runs | 10 ms | Not comparable across libraries below about 35 ms with the Phase 1 method |
243	| P2 | One series, 100,000 and 1,000,000 points, typed columns | Same | Median of 20 | 60 ms and 250 ms | See 01, section 5.3: uPlot and tuned Chart.js are the fastest measured |
244	| P3 | 600-point window, one appended point per display frame at 60 Hz, driven by an independent clock | Main-thread task time per update (per run: task time in the window divided by the number of updates); and frames later than 25 ms | Median over runs; count per 300 frames | 2 ms; 0 | See 01, section 5.3 |
245	| P4 | Pointer moved across a 100,000-point chart | Time from input to the frame showing the matching readout | 95th percentile | One frame (16.7 ms at 60 Hz) | Not measured in Phase 1 |
246	| P5 | 24 `trend` charts created on one page, each tile 340 × 200 px | Total main-thread task time | Median of 20 | 200 ms | Not measured in Phase 1 |
247	| P6 | Download | Minified and gzipped bytes, CSS included | Exact | DX7 | 01, section 1.4 |
248	| P8 | 40 compact charts created on one page, each 160 × 48 px with one series | Total main-thread task time | Median of 20 | 80 ms | Not measured in Phase 1 |
249	| P7 | Memory | Bytes owned by Axylo beyond the caller's columns for 1,000,000 points; heap trend over 1,000 create–destroy cycles | Measured after forced collection | At most 1.1× the size of the caller's two columns; no upward slope. (1.1× since 2026-11-18, the owner's decision: once a chart opened on typed columns is appended to, exact x, exact y and one state byte per point are 17 bytes per point against the caller's 16, measured 17,069,760 bytes for 1,000,000 points. Staying under 1× would need segmented columns, a contract change that was not judged worth it for 1.0.) | Not measured in Phase 1 |
250	
251	Three rules hold whatever the numbers become:
252	
253	1. Budgets apply to default options. Finding D8 showed libraries that are fast only for authors who know which options to set.
254	2. Budgets are absolute, on named hardware profiles. Comparison libraries, uPlot above all, are measured in the same runs and published beside Axylo, but a ratio to another library is not a gate: it would reward leaving out work that library does not do.
255	3. The accessibility layer is on in every measured configuration.
